{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793535249,"nonce":"u9cushazrauge253SI5/LUCB4bI=","title":"Small runs' peak RSS is mostly the executable's own pages, and moves with code layout","message":"NUC x86_64, zylua fib.lua sampled from /proc/PID/status every 2 ms: RssAnon 5 MB, RssFile 36 to 42 MB of a 41 to 47 MB peak. The file-backed part is pages of the 92 MB executable mapped while starting up; the kernel maps a window of cached pages around each fault (fault-around), so how many land in the process depends on the code layout and on how much of the file is in the page cache.\n\nConsequences seen while measuring:\n- Two builds differing only in a string-join helper in zrtl_string differ by 4 to 5 MB peak RSS on fib (RssFile 36 vs 41 MB) with identical RssAnon; json_bench 114 vs 116 MB with RssAnon 58 MB in both.\n- The same binary measured an hour apart read 38 MB and 46 to 48 MB on the memory-tight NUC (Firefox, 2.5 GB of swap in use): page cache state. Warming each binary (`cat bin \u003e /dev/null`) before measuring levels it.\n- A combined `cargo build -p zyntax_python -p zyntax_lua` unifies features across both, so its zylua is not the zylua a standalone build produces; comparing it to a standalone baseline showed a spurious 8% RSS gap.\n\nSo peak RSS comparisons between builds need RssAnon, or warmed binaries and the same build command; and the RSS columns against lua5.4 and LuaJIT count tens of MB of our own text. Options if it matters for the published numbers: madvise the text mapping MADV_RANDOM at start (no fault-around), or order the start-up path's functions together at link time.","files":null}]}